-- title:
--   Push–pull exchange for a linearly disjoint square of fields
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $F, A, B, E$ be fields equipped with $K$-algebra structures, with $B$ and $E$ satisfying `HasPrincipalDivisors` over $K$ (every nonzero element of the field is the divisor of a degree-zero divisor recording its orders at all places), this being the hypothesis required to form pullbacks of divisors to $B$ and to $E$. Given $K$-algebra maps $a : F \to A$, $b : F \to B$, $a' : A \to E$, $b' : B \to E$ whose underlying ring homomorphisms are integral, assume the square commutes, $b' \circ b = a' \circ a$; that $E$ is finite as a module over $F$ via $a' \circ a$ and separable over $F$ for that algebra structure; that the $K$-subalgebra of $E$ generated by the union of the ranges of $a'$ and $b'$ is all of $E$; and that the degrees satisfy $\operatorname{finrank}_F E = \operatorname{finrank}_F A \cdot \operatorname{finrank}_F B$, all ranks taken for the algebra structures induced by $a' \circ a$, $a$ and $b$ respectively. Then for every divisor $D$ on $A$, i.e. every finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on the places of $A$ over $K$, the pullback along $b$ of the pushforward along $a$ of $D$ equals the pushforward along $b'$ of the pullback along $a'$ of $D$, as divisors on $B$.
--
--   This is the divisor-level push–pull exchange identity for a commuting square of integral extensions of function fields whose upper field is generated by, and of degree equal to the product of the degrees of, the two intermediate fields (linear disjointness). It is the geometric input for the commutation of Hecke and diamond correspondences with degeneracy maps on Jacobians of modular curves, and is cited in that form by the statements about `heckeOperator`s and degeneracy pullbacks on $J_1$ and $J_H$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Divisor_pullbackAlong_pushforwardAlong_eq_pushforwardAlong_pullbackAlong.lean

